course: dictionary
lesson: 11793
title: Beneficence: doing good for others
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Beneficence is a formal word for kindness that does good. Its first part, bene, means good, the same as in benefit. So a person with beneficence is kind and generous, and their kindness helps others. «The school charity showed great beneficence by giving food to families in need.» The families were helped, so the charity did good.
explain: Beneficence is an uncountable noun, so we do not say "a beneficence" or "three beneficences". We talk about beneficence in general, or about an act of beneficence. «Her beneficence was known across the village, because she always helped people who were in trouble.» Here it describes a person's kind, generous nature.
explain: You will meet beneficence in ethics, religion and exam texts. In ethics it names a rule: act for the good of others. «Doctors follow the rule of beneficence: they must act for the good of their patients.» In religion, it can mean the goodness and kindness of God. «Christians believe in the beneficence of God.» Check the meaning by swapping in "doing good and being kind" and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Beneficence means doing good and being kind and generous to others.
fact: Beneficence is a formal word, so you will mostly read it in serious writing, not in everyday talk.
fact: «Beneficence» can also mean the goodness and kindness of God, or of a very generous person.
fact: An «act of beneficence» is a kind, generous deed that does good for someone.
fact: The part "bene" means good, like the start of the word benefit.
exercise: «The school charity showed great beneficence by giving food to families in need.» What does beneficence mean? | *Doing good and being kind to others | Working very hard in class | Losing things at school | Being angry with people
exercise: Sophie says beneficence is just a fancy word for being nice. Which answer is the most accurate? | *Beneficence means acting for the good of others, which is a more serious and generous kind of kindness | Beneficence means owning lots of things | Beneficence means being loud and cheerful | Beneficence means being tired after a long day
exercise: «Christians believe in the beneficence of God.» In this sentence, beneficence means | A holy book | A prayer said in a loud voice | *The goodness and kindness of God | A church bell
test: «Doctors follow the rule of beneficence: they must act for the good of their patients.» What is the rule of beneficence about? | Doctors working in the night | Doctors wearing white coats | *Acting for the good of others, especially patients | Doctors being paid well
test: Which phrase from the lesson means a kind, generous deed? | *an act of beneficence | a loud complaint | the beneficence of the rain | a very old castle
exam: Ali reads, «Her beneficence was known across the village.» What does beneficence most likely mean here? | Being tired and sleepy | Being rude to neighbours | *Being kind and generous to people | Being lost in the dark
